hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over the alakan fried  liver trap so the icon starts out with  e4  knight to f6 here pawn pushes forward to  attack the knight knight to d5 usually  don't just want to come back here to  g8 here pawn to d4  centralizing the pawns defending this  pawn here on e5  and then d6 this attacks this center  control that white has also opens up for  the light square bishop to get involved  into the game and white can either play  pawn to  c4 or the most popular move is just  knight to  f3 normal development move controlling  these central pawns with the knight and  black can either play the bishop down  here to g4 one of the reasons you play  d6 is to get your light square bishop  involved into the game or the second  most popular move is to play pawn to g6  preparing to fee and cater the bishop  here to  g7 now the name from frye liver comes in  very similar to if you've watched the  fried liver attack and that is  white playing the move knight to g5  saying yes i'm gonna go ahead and attack  the square here on f7 which early on is  one of the weakest squares for black  because it's only defended by the black  king and say hey i may even sacrifice  this knight here because i'm going to  have a very strong attack  even though i'm going to be down in  material so knight 2 g5 attacking the  pawn right here and black has a few  options as far as how they can continue  you know if they play bishop here to  g7 continue their normal thought process  then white has queen two f3  this is attacking queen up here taking  on f7 you could take with your knight  here on f7 attacking both the queen and  the rook here you're also attacking this  knight here on  d5 so lots of different ways that white  can attack here one of the things that  black could try to do is to play the  move pawn to h6 it is kind of breaking  up their  structure here on the king side as far  as the pawn protection but if they kind  of look at they can already recognize  that things are starting to blow up  anyway so they may just want to get this  knight out of here but this is exactly  what white wants to happen and white's  going to play the move knight takes here  on f7  as we discussed earlier black usually  wants to go ahead and take with their  king here on f7 if not we are forking  the queen and the rook here usually  don't just want to give us more material  here so king takes here on f7 and then  white plays the move queen to f3 this is  attacking the king so in check and also  attacking this knight here on  d5 now the best move from black is to  just play knight to f6 which looks kind  of weird because the pawn here on e5  can just take it and absolutely can but  that's the best move from black it's  it's very difficult i think to find  because after the pawn takes then we're  playing bishop here to c4 getting more  material involved into the game maybe  they play king to g7 uh now knight to c3  you can see white's getting all their  material involved this king is  completely exposed this rook over here  on a8 is not going to be doing anything  this bishop here on f8 is completely  blocked in  and white can just really start to pound  their opponent they can castle on the  queen's head at some point just throw  everything at the king side it's gonna  be very difficult for black to hold on  to this but that's really the best way  that black has to defend this and so i  think that one's going to be very  difficult for them to find  now the other option is for black to try  to hold on to this knight maybe  something like king 2 e6 just says yeah  i'm going to get out of the check and  i'm also going to try to protect this  knight here on  d5  now white has the move pawn to  c4 attacking this knight now how does  black continue from here well  it's actually correct to just still play  that knight to f6 but this is still  pretty awkward and i think it's very  difficult for black to find because  we're just going to go ahead and take  that knight here on f6 after the pawn  takes then we play bishop here to  d3 preparing to castle on the king side  you can see all the threats that we have  here the king's in the center of the  board even blocking off some of the  material here with the light square  bishop the queen can't get involved the  stark square bishop can't get involved  neither the rooks are involved and so  white can really just develop more  material and then start to push and they  always have moves like queen two d5 for  check they can always push forward with  the pawns here get their other bishop  involved into the game knight c3 adds a  lot of pressure as well so this is going  to be pretty tough for them to deal with  but there's another trap they could fall  into and that is if they play the move  knight to  b4 if they think that they're in harm's  way knight to b4 gets out of harm's way  and it also  prepares an attack here on knight to c2  and this is check and it's also forking  this rook here on a1  if black does play this and fall into  the trap this is going to be a quick  checkmate no way to stop it after the  move pawn to  d5 check if you look at it black can't  take here on e5 because just queen to f4  that's going to be checkmate the king  can't come to the ephile just because  the queen is defending that and so if  the king comes back here to d7 well  pawnee six king comes back here and  that's gonna be checkmate as well and if  they try to just take this pawn here  with their knight if they recognize hey  i should just give back this knight  which they should have done a long time  ago then white can go ahead and take  here on d5 after the king takes here on  e5 uh then a little bit different move  uh than before but now we're gonna be  playing the move pawn to b4 preparing to  play the move bishop here to b2 and  there's no way to really stop this you  know bishop here to  f5 as an example but then just bishop  here to  b2 and you can see this is checkmate  kind of a weird checkmate with king in  the center of the board and really just  the the bishop giving the check but the  pawn here blocking off being protected  by the queen  and just defending all of the squares  that black would want to go to now black  could delay this a little bit by playing  the move knight to c6 only reason they  would do that is the knight can now come  to d4 to protect from the check if this  is the case why it's going to change it  up just slightly and they're going to  play the move  queen 2 f4 check  then king over here takes on d5 but then  the move bishop here to c4 and this is  going to be checkmate so no way to stop  it uh from black once they play that  move knight to  b4 uh  preparing to come down and fork our king  they're not going to have that chance  after we play d5 it is going to be  checkmate so that is the alekhine fried  liver  always be thinking of ways if you come  back to the beginning of this how you  can play your knights here to  g5 because threatening this square here  on f7  many times the exchange is going to be  worth it because you can start to launch  a very very powerful attack on your  opponent this shows that the fried liver  can be effective even in the alekhine  defense which many times is known to be  very very solid from black once you  expose that king it is off to the races  so thank you guys so much for watching  this video i know many of you really  love these traps give you a different  perspective on how to think about chess  and some nuances around when someone  plays in a certain way how you can start  to attack your opponents thank you guys  for watching the video i'll see you guys  next time  you

